  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Home alone: Remote work, isolation, and mental health (2026) in Science. 1 PubPeer thread(s).</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Home alone: Remote work, isolation, and mental health (2026) in Science is authored by Natalia Emanuel, Emma Harrington and Amanda Pallais. Topics: Mental health, Affect (linguistics), Isolation (microbiology), Work (physics) and Mental distress.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">6 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include American Journal of Roentgenology, Acta Psychologica and Science.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">It is discussed on Bluesky (9 mentions). 9 online mentions.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This article is already drawing an early scholarly response: it has 6 citations, 7 documented downstream reuse events, and 1 PubPeer thread, suggesting both uptake and post-publication scrutiny. The clearest pattern in the record is that other researchers are using its findings to frame related work on work-from-home arrangements, work loneliness, disability, health, and organizational well-being.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Home alone: Remote work, isolation, and mental health tackles a timely question with a large, nationally representative evidence base, drawing on five U.S. surveys spanning 2011 to 2024 and using a difference-in-differences design to compare remotable and nonremotable jobs. The abstract reports that remote work is associated with more time spent alone, worse mental well-being across multiple measures, and greater use of mental health services, making the paper especially relevant to ongoing debates about the long-run social costs of remote work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s earliest impact is visible in citations and reuse rather than in broad public discussion. OpenAlex records 6 citations, all in 2026 so far, and the citing literature is spreading across venues and topics tied to work-from-home, technostress, work-family balance, workplace health, and even discipline-specific discussions in health and education. That pattern suggests the paper is being read as a reference point for multiple adjacent conversations rather than remaining confined to one field.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also evidence of practical and pedagogical uptake. A YouTube video explicitly links the paper in its metadata, and the article has been reused in connection with a Dryad dataset and in other downstream scholarly items, including a preprint and a biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. These signals indicate that the study is not only being cited but also entering broader workflows where its framing or results are reused for new questions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record is still early, and the public-facing footprint is comparatively modest: one blog mention appears in the web record, while event-data coverage is unavailable from the archive. At the same time, the presence of one PubPeer thread shows that the article has attracted post-publication attention and scrutiny. Taken together, the evidence points to a work that is already influential in scholarly and applied discussions of remote work, even as its longer-term impact continues to develop.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
